{"data":[{"id":"u69mg_v3","type":"preprints","attributes":{"date_created":"2026-10-19T04:29:16.778893","date_modified":"2026-10-19T14:27:14.516658","date_published":"2026-10-19T14:27:14.491914","original_publication_date":null,"custom_publication_citation":null,"doi":null,"title":"Color naming needs a non-uniform perceptual prior: Individual differences in hue scaling and the vision–language interface","description":"How a language divides the color spectrum into named categories is increasingly explained as efficient compression: a vocabulary is a code that names colors as accurately as possible with as few words as possible. Every such model needs a description of how similar colors look, and nearly all use the same one: a standard, evenly spaced color space (CIE L*a*b*, or CIELAB) in which each color carries a fixed, symmetric blur of perceptual uncertainty. Here I argue that this assumption is wrong in a way that matters. When people with normal color vision judge hues they disagree in systematic ways, and the pattern of that disagreement, summarized by factor analysis, points to roughly eight narrow, largely independent regions that tile the color circle and lie near category boundaries rather than their best examples. I show how to build this measured structure into the standard model by stretching the circle at the category boundaries. A full test substitutes the reshaped space into the information-bottleneck model and rescores all 110 World Color Survey languages. Under the model’s near-uniform default prior the warp is close to a wash; but under a realistic, measured communicative need a modest warp improves fit for most languages, a small but systematic benefit the uniform prior masked, although the predicted optimal category systems themselves barely change. The effect is small and conditional, marking when a non-uniform perceptual space matters. The proposal yields a prediction testable with public data and code, informative whether it succeeds or fails.","is_published":true,"is_preprint_orphan":false,"license_record":{"copyright_holders":[],"year":null},"tags":["color categories","Color naming","communicative efficiency","factor analysis","hue scaling","individual differences","information bottleneck"],"preprint_doi_created":null,"date_withdrawn":null,"current_user_permissions":[],"public":true,"reviews_state":"accepted","date_last_transitioned":"2026-10-19T14:27:14.491914","version":3,"is_latest_version":true,"has_coi":false,"conflict_of_interest_statement":null,"has_data_links":"not_applicable","why_no_data":null,"data_links":[],"has_prereg_links":"not_applicable","why_no_prereg":null,"prereg_links":[],"prereg_link_info":null,"default_license_id":"","subjects":[[{"id":"5b4e7425c6983001430b6c1b","text":"Neuroscience"}],[{"id":"5b4e7425c6983001430b6c21","text":"Life Sciences"}],[{"id":"5b4e7425c6983001430b6c1e","text":"Social and Behavioral Sciences"},{"id":"5b4e7426c6983001430b6c41","text":"Quantitative Methods"}],[{"id":"5b4e7425c6983001430b6c1e","text":"Social and Behavioral Sciences"},{"id":"5b4e7426c6983001430b6c62","text":"Linguistics"}],[{"id":"5b4e7425c6983001430b6c1e","text":"Social and Behavioral Sciences"},{"id":"5b4e7427c6983001430b6c71","text":"Perception"}],[{"id":"5b4e7425c6983001430b6c1e","text":"Social and Behavioral Sciences"},{"id":"5b4e7427c6983001430b6c7d","text":"Physiology"}]]},"relationships":{"contributors":{"links":{"related":{"href":"https://api.osf.io/v2/preprints/u69mg_v3/contributors/","meta":{}}}},"bibliographic_contributors":{"links":{"related":{"href":"https://api.osf.io/v2/preprints/u69mg_v3/bibliographic_contributors/","meta":{}}}},"versions":{"links":{"related":{"href":"https://api.osf.io/v2/preprints/u69mg_v3/versions/","meta":{}}}},"citation":{"links":{"related":{"href":"https://api.osf.io/v2/preprints/u69mg_v3/citation/","meta":{}}},"data":{"id":"u69mg_v3","type":"citation"}},"identifiers":{"links":{"related":{"href":"https://api.osf.io/v2/preprints/u69mg_v3/identifiers/","me